Caching System Design Notes

Core Concepts:

Definition: Caching stores copies of data in a temporary storage location to provide faster access than the original source.
The 80/20 Rule: Caching is effective because 80% of traffic is typically generated by 20% of the data.

Cache Hit vs. Cache Miss:
  Hit: Data is found in the cache and returned immediately.
  Miss: Data is not in the cache, requiring a fetch from the underlying database, which is significantly slower.

Caching Layers
  Browser/Client Cache: Lives on the user device for static assets. Fastest access (0 latency).
  CDN Cache: Distributed edge servers for static files and media.
  Application Cache: Dedicated servers (e.g., Redis, Memcached) inside the data center for query results and session state.
  Database Query Cache: Memory within the database server to avoid redundant disk reads.

Caching Strategies
  Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading): The application checks the cache first. If a miss occurs, it loads data from the database into the cache.
  Write-Through: Data is written to both the cache and the database simultaneously, ensuring strong consistency but slower writes.
  Write-Behind (Write-Back): Data is written only to the cache, with an asynchronous background worker flushing updates to the database later. Offers high performance but carries a risk of data loss.

Eviction Policies
  LRU (Least Recently Used): Removes the item that has not been accessed for the longest time.
  FIFO (First-In, First-Out): Removes the oldest entries first.
  TTL (Time to Live): Entries are automatically removed after a set duration.
  Random: Evicts an arbitrary item; rarely used unless access is uniform.

Invalidation Strategies
  TTL-based: Simple but risks stale data until expiration.
  Event-based (Active Invalidation): The application explicitly deletes or updates the cache entry when the source data changes.
  Hybrid: Combines TTL and event-based methods for better control.

Failure Modes & Mitigations
  Cache Stampede: Multiple requests hit the database simultaneously when a popular key expires. Mitigation: Use mutex locks or early refreshes.
  Cache Penetration: Requests for data that does not exist constantly bypass the cache. Mitigation: Cache negative results (nulls).
  Cache Avalanche: A large number of cache keys expire at the same time. Mitigation: Add random jitters to TTL values.

Best Practices
  Do not cache data that requires high consistency (e.g., bank balances).
  Cache as close to the user as possible.
  Always warm up your cache after system restarts.
  Monitor eviction rates to ensure the cache size is appropriate.
